2026-11-07T00:00:03Z (RFD66606-2461) 'Does skipping SQLite's bytecode engine on the store's hot read paths buy a real win once the read side is already network-bound?
 # tags: rfd:hook
 Text:RFD2461  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:04Z (RFD66606-2461) 'The same outside challenge entry that inspired the compaction thread also walks SQLite's B-tree cursors directly inside a read transaction, skipping the SQL engine and its column copies, and decoding record bytes by hand with a SQL fallback kept for safety. The store's reads already go through prepare and step today. The honest read is that this is a constant-factor win, not a network round-trip win: the store's existing speculative reads and read-ahead already save roughly a round trip per read and turn two hundred fifty six pages into about two round trips, so the entry's trick can add at most something like five to fifteen percent, and only if profiling shows the SQL engine's overhead is real.
 # tags: rfd:intrigue
 Text:RFD2461  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:05Z (RFD66606-2461) 'Profile the hot read paths first; only build the cursor-based reader if profiling shows engine overhead worth the complexity, and keep the existing speculative read and read-ahead engineering untouched either way.
 # tags: rfd:exit
 Text:RFD2461  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:06Z (RFD66606-2461) '- Profile the store's hot read paths before writing any cursor-reading code, to confirm whether the SQL engine's prepare and step overhead is actually a measurable share of read latency.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2461  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-2461) '- If profiling supports it, add a cursor-based reader that walks the B-tree directly inside a read transaction and decodes record bytes by hand, with the existing SQL path kept as a fallback rather than removed.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2461  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-2461) '- Treat any win here as a constant-factor improvement on top of the already network-bound read path, not a replacement for the speculative-read and read-ahead work already in place.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2461  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-11-07T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-2461) '- Do not spend further effort on this transfer unless the profiling step above actually shows engine overhead large enough to matter.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2461  1 LINE
